### What is internal exactly-once in Flink?

Internally, exactly-once state is guaranteed by checkpointing. Barriers flow through the graph, operators snapshot a consistent cut, and sources record their offsets. On recovery, Flink restores state and rewinds sources so each record updates state exactly once.

```java
package com.bank.streaming;

import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.environment.StreamExecutionEnvironment;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.environment.CheckpointConfig;

public class InternalExactlyOnceDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        StreamExecutionEnvironment env =
            StreamExecutionEnvironment.getExecutionEnvironment();

        // Enable checkpointing for internal exactly-once
        env.enableCheckpointing(30_000); // every 30 seconds
        env.getCheckpointConfig().setCheckpointingMode(
            CheckpointingMode.EXACTLY_ONCE);
        env.getCheckpointConfig().setCheckpointStorage(
            "s3://nbi-checkpoints/flink/");

        env.addSource(new KafkaSource<>("nbi-transactions"))
            .keyBy(Transaction::getAccountId)
            .process(new AccountBalanceTracker()) // keyed state
            .print();

        // On failure: restore state + rewind Kafka offsets
        // Each record updates state exactly once
        env.execute("NBI Internal Exactly-Once Demo");
    }
}
```

### Why is internal exactly-once not enough?

Internal consistency means operator state is correct, but the sink writes to the outside world. If the sink wrote output as it processed and the job then failed and replayed, the replayed records would be written again, producing duplicates in the external system.

```text
Problem without transactional sink:

  1. Flink processes records R1, R2, R3
  2. Sink writes R1, R2, R3 to external database
  3. Crash before checkpoint completes
  4. Recovery: Flink replays from last checkpoint
  5. Sink writes R1, R2, R3 AGAIN
  6. External database now has R1, R2, R3 TWICE

Solution: sink participates in checkpoint protocol
```

### How does the two-phase commit protocol work?

The two-phase commit ties the external transaction to the checkpoint lifecycle. It has four hooks: beginTransaction opens a new transaction, preCommit prepares it when the barrier arrives, commit runs only after checkpoint confirmation, and abort rolls back on failure.

```java
package com.bank.streaming;

import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.functions.sink
    .TwoPhaseCommitSinkFunction;

public class ExactlyOnceLedgerSink
        extends TwoPhaseCommitSinkFunction<Transaction,
            JdbcTransaction, Void> {

    @Override
    protected JdbcTransaction beginTransaction() {
        // Open new JDBC transaction
        Connection conn = dataSource.getConnection();
        conn.setAutoCommit(false);
        return new JdbcTransaction(conn);
    }

    @Override
    protected void invoke(JdbcTransaction txn, Transaction record,
            Context context) throws Exception {
        // Buffer writes into the open transaction
        PreparedStatement ps = txn.getConnection().prepareStatement(
            "INSERT INTO nbi_ledger (account_id, amount, txn_time) "
            + "VALUES (?, ?, ?)");
        ps.setString(1, record.getAccountId());
        ps.setBigDecimal(2, record.getAmount());
        ps.setTimestamp(3, record.getTxnTime());
        ps.executeUpdate();
    }

    @Override
    protected void preCommit(JdbcTransaction txn) {
        // Flush and prepare but do NOT commit
        // Called when checkpoint barrier reaches the sink
        txn.getConnection().flush();
    }

    @Override
    protected void commit(JdbcTransaction txn) {
        // Called ONLY when checkpoint is globally confirmed
        txn.getConnection().commit();
        txn.getConnection().close();
    }

    @Override
    protected void abort(JdbcTransaction txn) {
        // Rollback on failure
        txn.getConnection().rollback();
        txn.getConnection().close();
    }
}
```

### How does the Kafka sink achieve exactly-once?

The Kafka sink uses Kafka's transactional producer. Pre-committed records are invisible to consumers reading in `read_committed` mode until the commit lands. The `transactional.id` prefix links the Flink checkpoint to the Kafka transaction.

```java
package com.bank.streaming;

import org.apache.flink.connector.kafka.sink.KafkaSink;
import org.apache.flink.connector.kafka.sink.KafkaRecordSerializationSchema;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.environment.StreamExecutionEnvironment;
import org.apache.flink.connector.base.DeliveryGuarantee;

public class KafkaExactlyOnceDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        StreamExecutionEnvironment env =
            StreamExecutionEnvironment.getExecutionEnvironment();
        env.enableCheckpointing(30_000);

        KafkaSink<Transaction> sink = KafkaSink.<Transaction>builder()
            .setBootstrapServers("kafka:9092")
            .setRecordSerializer(
                KafkaRecordSerializationSchema.builder()
                    .setTopic("nbi-settlements")
                    .setValueSerializationSchema(
                        new TransactionSerializer())
                    .build())
            .setDeliveryGuarantee(DeliveryGuarantee.EXACTLY_ONCE)
            .setTransactionalIdPrefix("nbi-settlement-sink")
            .build();

        env.addSource(new KafkaSource<>("nbi-transactions"))
            .keyBy(Transaction::getAccountId)
            .process(new SettlementCalculator())
            .sinkTo(sink);

        env.execute("NBI Kafka Exactly-Once Demo");
    }
}
```

### What is the visibility latency with exactly-once sinks?

Downstream readers only see output after the checkpoint completes and the commit lands. The checkpoint interval becomes the end-to-end latency floor for exactly-once output.

```text
Visibility timeline:

  t=0s     Checkpoint interval starts
  t=0-30s  Sink processes records into open Kafka transaction
           Records INVISIBLE to read_committed consumers
  t=30s    Checkpoint barrier reaches sink --> preCommit
  t=31s    Checkpoint confirmed by JobManager --> commit
           Records NOW VISIBLE to consumers

  Visibility latency = checkpoint interval + checkpoint duration
  With 30s interval: consumers see data ~30-35s after production
```

```java
// Trade-off: lower checkpoint interval = lower latency but more overhead
env.enableCheckpointing(10_000);  // 10s: lower latency, more checkpoints
env.enableCheckpointing(60_000);  // 60s: higher latency, fewer checkpoints
// Choose based on business latency requirements vs checkpoint cost
```

### What about idempotent sinks as an alternative?

If the external system cannot do transactions, you can achieve effectively-once with an idempotent sink that uses a deterministic ID. Replays overwrite rather than duplicate.

```java
package com.bank.streaming;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class IdempotentSinkDemo {
    // Deterministic ID based on record content
    // Same record always produces same ID
    public static String deterministicId(Transaction txn) {
        return txn.getAccountId() + "-" + txn.getTxnTime().getTime()
            + "-" + txn.getAmount().toPlainString();
    }

    // Idempotent upsert: INSERT ... ON CONFLICT UPDATE
    // Replays overwrite the same row, no duplicates
    // "INSERT INTO nbi_ledger (txn_id, account_id, amount) "
    // "VALUES (?, ?, ?) "
    // "ON CONFLICT (txn_id) DO UPDATE SET amount = EXCLUDED.amount"
}
```

### What are common mistakes with exactly-once sinks?

The most common mistake is not setting the Kafka `transaction.timeout.ms` higher than the maximum checkpoint interval plus recovery time. If the Kafka transaction expires before Flink can commit it after recovery, the data is lost.

```java
// WRONG: default Kafka transaction timeout (15 min)
// Checkpoint interval: 5 min, recovery time: up to 20 min
// If recovery takes 20 min, transaction expires before commit!

// CORRECT: set transaction timeout > checkpoint interval + recovery
KafkaSink.<Transaction>builder()
    .setProperty("transaction.timeout.ms", "1800000") // 30 min
    .setDeliveryGuarantee(DeliveryGuarantee.EXACTLY_ONCE)
    .setTransactionalIdPrefix("nbi-settlement-sink")
    .build();
// Also ensure Kafka broker: transaction.max.timeout.ms >= 1800000
```

### What should a senior engineer know about exactly-once?

A senior engineer should know that exactly-once is a contract across the whole pipeline: a source that cannot replay (non-replayable) or a sink that cannot transact or deduplicate breaks it regardless of Flink's internal guarantees. They should also know the end-to-end latency trade-off and the transaction timeout pitfall.

```text
Exactly-once requirements:
  Source:  Must be replayable (Kafka, Kinesis, files)
  State:   Checkpointed with EXACTLY_ONCE mode
  Sink:    Must be transactional (2PC) or idempotent

  If ANY component breaks the chain:
  Source not replayable?    --> at-most-once possible
  Sink not transactional?   --> at-least-once with duplicates
  Checkpointing disabled?   --> no recovery guarantees
```

```java
package com.bank.streaming;

import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.environment.StreamExecutionEnvironment;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.environment.CheckpointConfig;

public class SeniorExactlyOnceConfig {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        StreamExecutionEnvironment env =
            StreamExecutionEnvironment.getExecutionEnvironment();

        // Complete exactly-once configuration
        env.enableCheckpointing(30_000);
        CheckpointConfig cfg = env.getCheckpointConfig();
        cfg.setCheckpointingMode(CheckpointingMode.EXACTLY_ONCE);
        cfg.setMinPauseBetweenCheckpoints(10_000);
        cfg.setCheckpointTimeout(120_000);
        cfg.setCheckpointStorage("s3://nbi-checkpoints/");
        cfg.setExternalizedCheckpointCleanup(
            CheckpointConfig.ExternalizedCheckpointCleanup
                .RETAIN_ON_CANCELLATION);

        // Source: Kafka (replayable)
        // Sink: Kafka with EXACTLY_ONCE + transactional ID
        // Full end-to-end exactly-once chain
        env.execute("NBI Senior Exactly-Once Config");
    }
}
```
